          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-be393a6a3d4f0015\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":4,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.98,\"analysis\":\"Nikki’s utterance is both a sincere avowal of her loving state (T) and a reflex-like expressive discharge (E). The narration explicitly presents the words as suddenly spilling out before she can inhibit them, providing independent evidence for E beyond emotional intensity. It does not substantially undertake or renew a commitment, so P receives no support.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The declaration has relational significance, but the passage frames it as an honest admission and affect-driven release rather than a commitment or promise.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Nikki directly says “I love you” to Keith while they are lying together after sex.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The declaration occurs amid tenderness and imminent separation after an emotionally intense encounter. Nikki’s full heart supports genuine loving affect, while “said suddenly” and “spilling out before she could stop them” make the verbal production itself reflex-like. The subsequent description of the admission as a release and a moment of pure honesty reinforces both E and T. Keith reciprocates, but Nikki’s utterance itself does not explicitly commit her to a future course of action or relationship undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“She turned to him, her heart full. ‘I love you,’ she said suddenly”\",\"supports\":\"Nikki directly avows a loving emotional state, strongly supporting T; the sudden delivery also contributes to E when read with the following narration.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“the words spilling out before she could stop them”\",\"supports\":\"Provides explicit positive evidence that the words function as an involuntary or reflex-like affective discharge, strongly supporting E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“The admission felt like a release, a moment of pure honesty”\",\"supports\":\"The narration characterizes the utterance as a truthful admission and a release, independently reinforcing both T and E.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Keith replies, “I love you too,” and the exchange leaves both characters “vulnerable but deeply connected.”\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the participants understand Nikki’s words as a genuine interpersonal love declaration rather than an instrumental or semantically empty use.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as an affirmative present-tense explicit “I love you” construction and retained as VALID_EXPLICIT_LOVE_I_YOU.\",\"supports\":\"Corroborates identification of the highlighted core utterance as a direct, explicit love declaration.\",\"confidence\":1.0}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T and E jointly capture the core force: a loving state is avowed, and that state is presented as directly producing the words. Contextual intimacy, vulnerability, and relational consequences require no additional core category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
